WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.970
For the most part, the cast of Avengers: Endgame
was in the dark just as much as the fans when

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:06.780
it came to the movie's plot developments.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:10.360
Here's how they reacted when they got the
news about what would be happening to Earth's

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.400
Mightiest Heroes in the war against Thanos.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:17.240
One of the most crowd-pleasing moments of
Avengers: Endgame comes when Tony Stark channels

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.440
Black Sabbath one last time before wiping
out Thanos and his forces with the Infinity

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:21.940
Stones.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:23.020
"I am Iron Man."

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.840
The move wins the day for the good guys with
a fantastic callback, bringing the series

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:30.460
full circle in what some fans would call a
"crowning moment of awesome."

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:33.780
It's just a perfect line for Tony, and one
that wasn't in the script.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:38.240
After Endgame was released, directors Anthony
and Joe Russo revealed the line was added

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:43.280
to the movie in reshoots, after editor Jeff
Ford realized a response was needed for Thanos'

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.280
final declaration of "I am inevitable."

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.660
The Russos brought Downey to dinner to pitch
the reshoot, with Hollywood super-producer

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.680
Joel Silver also tagging along.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.060
According to the Russos, Downey's reaction
to his character's death was so powerful that

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:58.320
he resisted being brought back to face it
again.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.260
They recalled him being hesitant, saying.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:01.680
"I don't know.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.700
I don't really want to go back and get into
that emotional state."

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:08.480
"Just when I thought I was out, they pull
me back in."

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:12.800
It was apparently Silver an old friend of
Downey's who managed to change his mind.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.900
According to the Russos, he responded to the
actor's doubts with,

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.439
"Robert, what are you talking about?

00:01:17.439 --> 00:01:19.280
That's the greatest line I've ever heard!

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.399
You gotta say this line!

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:21.539
You have to do this!"

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.300
And like a snap, it was done.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:25.000
"How 'bout that?"

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:30.940
Speaking to Jimmy Fallon on The Tonight Show,
Winter Soldier actor Sebastian Stan discussed

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:34.900
how tight the secrecy was while filming Endgame
even more so than usual.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:38.700
When asked if he ever had any idea of what
exactly he was filming, Stan said,

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.240
"Not for this one we didn't.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:44.450
I mean, everything I filmed I found out on
the day."

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:48.320
He also joked that he concealed his confusion
with some of that smoldering Winter Soldier

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:48.860
charisma.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:54.620
"You can kinda tell behind my expression,
I hide the confusion well, under a Zoolander

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:55.120
look."

00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:00.039
Stan referred specifically to one of Endgame's
final scenes when the surviving heroes gather

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:01.520
for Tony Stark's memorial.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.600
According to Stan, he was initially told it
was a very different type of ceremony.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:10.780
"I was told it was a wedding, that there was
a wedding that we were filming.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:11.880
And I was like, 'Who's getting married?'"

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:16.660
Throughout the Guardians of the Galaxy movies,
Drax has demonstrated that you don't need

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:18.520
a lot of words to say how you really feel.

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:23.260
Dave Bautista proved the same point with his
brief but telling response to Avengers: Endgame.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:25.980
In an April 29 post, the Drax actor tweeted,

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:27.980
"Annnnnd...still processing."

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.380
Drax doesn't appear in Endgame until the final
battle, so considering the secrecy and misdirection

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:37.831
used on actors like Mark Ruffalo, whose characters
were more important to the overall plot, it

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:41.980
isn't likely Bautista knew much about what
happened in the movie outside scenes he filmed.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:45.380
His reactions were likely just like the ones
felt by most of the audience.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:49.720
His "processing" may also revolve around what
it all means for Drax's future in the MCU.

00:02:49.739 --> 00:02:54.430
With the death of Thanos a death which, let's
remember, has very little to do with Drax's

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:57.880
actions in the film Bautista's character's
chief motivation is gone.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:02:58.880
"Yes.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.160
Of course, Ronan was only a puppet.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:04.480
It's really Thanos I need to kill."

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.480
Then there's the fact that, at the end of
Endgame, Thor seemed to be joining the cast

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:10.420
of Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 3.

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:14.620
As such, it's safe to imagine that at least
some of Bautista's processing might be of

00:03:14.629 --> 00:03:16.150
a professional nature.

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:18.990
After all, Drax is a big strong guy who says
funny things.

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:21.440
Thor is a big strong guy who says funny things.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:22.540
Do you really need two?

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.769
Then again, maybe he's just processing that,
in the next film, he gets to work in such

00:03:26.769 --> 00:03:28.320
a beautiful creature's presence:

00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:29.520
"He is not a dude.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:31.160
You're a dude.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:34.960
This, this is a man.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.200
A handsome, muscular man."

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:42.180
Leave it to Star-Lord to do the exact opposite
of what he's supposed to do.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:44.320
"What are you doing?"

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:46.340
"Dance off, bro!

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:47.420
Me and you!"

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:54.819
On the set of Endgame, while taking some downtime
during filming the movie's climactic final

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:59.599
battle, Chris Pratt took out his phone and
knowingly broke all the rules he started recording.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.200
"This is a really illegal video."

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:06.940
The star posted the video to his Instagram
account on May first, and it's a regular who's

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:11.129
who of MCU actors, folks who are now some
of the most famous performers on the planet.

00:04:11.129 --> 00:04:14.580
When it came to one of the biggest moments
in the movie, Pratt's first thought was to

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:18.650
make sure, quote, "no rule was going to stop"
him from capturing and sharing it.

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:20.000
And it's kind of tough to blame him.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:23.040
Chris Evans ends the video by playfully telling
his costar that he's in:

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:24.360
"Big trouble."

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:25.420
"Big time."

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:31.260
But it's Sean Gunn, who motion-captures Rocket
Raccoon, who has the best line in the clip:

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:34.200
"I'm pretty sure I'm the most famous person
here."

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:39.000
One of Endgame's biggest moments didn't take
place on the screen or behind the cameras;

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.280
it happened in the record books.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:45.590
In early May, Avengers: Endgame knocked 1997's
Titanic out of the #2 spot on the list of

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:48.020
highest-grossing films in box office history.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:52.560
The development didn't go unnoticed by Titanic
director James Cameron, who issued a social

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:54.680
media congratulations to the MCU.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:59.280
Posting a picture on Instagram of a ship sunk
by a half-submerged Avengers logo rather than

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.380
an iceberg, Cameron accompanied the image
with:

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:03.760
"An iceberg to sank the real Titanic.

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:07.590
It took The Avengers to sink my Titanic…You've
shown that not only is the movie industry

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:08.590
alive and well.

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:09.580
It's bigger than ever!"

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:14.380
Don Cheadle, who plays War Machine, responded
to Cameron's classy congrats with a now-deleted

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:18.540
tweet that read, "Nothing personal, James…it's
the fans," and a shrugging emoji.

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:22.780
Judging by the responses Cheadle's tweet received,
the snark has not been overlooked.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:27.520
Cameron rubbed superhero movie fans the wrong
way in April 2018, when he told Indiewire

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:30.520
he was hoping for what he called, quote, "Avenger
fatigue."

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:32.360
Cameron said he liked the movies, but

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:36.600
"...there are other stories to tell besides
hyper-gonadal males without families doing

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:40.200
death-defying things for two hours and wrecking
cities in the process."

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:44.200
Perhaps now Cameron is relieved Endgame has
come and gone, so that those "hyper-gonadal

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:48.440
males" will leave for a bit and give Cameron
a chance to show us some Terminator movies

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:50.420
with, uh, Arnold Schwarzenegger.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:55.240
Downey, Bautista, and Cheadle all showed us
how only a few words can say so much.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:59.630
Hawkeye actor Jeremy Renner went a bit further,
and reinforced that old adage about a picture

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:01.840
and how many stories it can tell on its own.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:04.980
Renner's mother accompanied him to the Avengers:
Endgame premiere.

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:08.960
The next day, Renner posted a touching photo
of himself holding his mother and appearing

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:11.180
very emotional; perhaps even in tears.

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:12.060
Renner wrote:

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:16.520
"Wow…words will only betray my feelings
for this movie…this picture from last night

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:21.160
represents…love…pride of my family, my
friends, and crazy for my avenger family."

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:25.040
Renner didn't mention any specific scenes
or aspects of the film that put him in such

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:29.210
a vulnerable state, but anyone who has seen
Endgame probably has an easy time imagining

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:32.760
more than a few things that could leave the
Avengers' favorite archer in tears.

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:38.060
One of the biggest moments of Avengers: Endgame's
resolution is when the aged Steve Rogers passes

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:39.060
his shield to Falcon.

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:42.160
It's a welcome, crowd-pleasing moment toward
the end of the film.

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:45.600
Apparently, Anthonie Mackie was one of the
last to know it was going to happen.

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:49.380
Fittingly, Mackie said the moment he learned
about it was an interesting reflection of

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:50.420
what we see on the screen.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:51.740
Mackie told IMDb:

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:53.420
"I didn't even know it was happening.

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:56.200
We were at his house and he goes, 'You excited?'

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:58.040
I went, 'What you talking about?'

00:06:58.040 --> 00:06:59.300
He goes, 'Wait, you don’t know?'"

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:03.240
At that point, Mackie says Evans ran out of
the room and returned with the script for

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:04.080
Mackie to read.

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:11.040
"And I read the scene and I’m like, ‘Oh,’
and he goes, ‘Yes' We cried, we drank, we

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:12.000
laughed, it became this thing."

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:16.300
He went on to express his gratitude that he
got to spend that kind of moment with Evans,

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:20.000
and it sounds like he got a good laugh out
of getting the news later than everyone else.

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:21.960
"Don’t look at me.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:23.620
I do what he does, just slower."

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:27.840
Anthony Mackie may have been grateful for
the moment he had with Chris Evans where he

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:31.400
was passed Cap's shield, but a lot of fans
were the exact opposite of grateful when the

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:35.750
Captain America actor talked about his reaction
to Avengers: Endgame with The Hollywood Reporter

00:07:35.750 --> 00:07:37.880
a month before the film's theatrical release.

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:41.480
Evans told THR he, quote, "choked up like
three times."

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:45.710
A month later, at Endgame's L.A. premiere,
Evans boosted that number to six.

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:49.590
The original THR interview upset fans about
the prospect of being upset.

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:53.340
The news that the movie made one of the stars
cry had some asking how they were to cope.

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:55.180
"I'm very upset!

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:57.360
You know what I like about being upset?

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:57.860
Blame!"

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:02.320
What really set the fans on fire was Evans'
joke during the THR interview about his own

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:03.970
character's supposed death.

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:05.660
The interview ends with Evans joking,

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:06.540
"It's hard.

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:07.240
Seeing my own death.

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:09.300
It's going to be a long movie, that's for
sure.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:11.290
The first edit clocked in over three hours.

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:12.540
My funeral's like an hour."

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:16.800
Some fans went so far as to direct some less
than polite words at the actor for joking

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:20.300
about Cap dying, but plenty of others had
a good sense of humor about it.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:25.300
Along with big moments that happened in Endgame,
we've also seen some fun reactions to moments

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:29.210
that fortunately (or unfortunately, depending
on your tastes) didn't happen.

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:33.700
Like the so-called "ThanusTheory," which guessed
that Ant-Man would defeat Thanos by shrinking,

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:37.060
entering the Mad Titan's butt, and then switching
to Giant Man size.

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:41.580
UK talk show host Graham Norton asked Paul
Rudd about the theory, and both Norton and

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:44.610
fellow guest Chris Hemsworth pointed out the
tactic would work.

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:47.000
But apparently not in love with the idea,
Rudd asked,

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:50.560
"Why not the ear or the nose?

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:52.660
I mean, why does it have to be…?"

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:58.090
The Titan himself got to comment on the theory
when Variety asked Josh Brolin about it at

00:08:58.090 --> 00:08:59.140
the Endgame premiere.

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:03.500
Referring to an Instagram post he'd made earlier
that day featuring an artist's rendering of

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:08.810
naked Thanos' backside, Brolin dodged addressing
the theory directly, instead choosing to celebrate

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:10.600
the Mad Titan’s impressive physique.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:13.320
"That's a peach, man, that's a peach.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:17.680
That's a purple peach if I've ever seen one,
that's a beautiful, purple peach."

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:22.540
In the aftermath of Endgame's release, one
of the film's most polarizing aspects was

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:24.600
the appearance of so-called "Fat Thor."

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:28.930
Five years after the Decimation and Thanos'
death at the hands of Thor, the thunder god

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:31.280
is revealed to be in dire emotional straits.

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:35.510
Thor is depicted as having done little for
the past few years but eat, drink heavily,

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:36.300
and play video games.

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:38.100
"Thor!"

00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:39.500
"Is he asleep?"

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:44.100
Regardless of the debate about the character's
Endgame trajectory and how it was handled,

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:46.500
we know now that Hemsworth was excited about
it.

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:51.030
In an interview with Sirius XM, directors
Joe and Anthony Russo shared how they pitched

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:53.520
Hemsworth their plans over the phone for Fat
Thor.

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:55.020
Anthony said during the call,

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:59.400
"[Hemsworth] was so energized by the work
he had done [in Thor:Ragnarok] in terms of

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:01.620
coming up with a more comedic and irreverent
Thor."

00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:03.880
That's when Joe Russo chimed in, saying,

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:06.000
"'Wait until you hear what we've got for you.'"

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:07.420
"I think I'm having a panic attack."

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:08.620
"Get it together!"

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:13.360
Considering Hemsworth has said he was bored
with playing Thor before Ragnarok re-energized

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:17.650
his interest, it's a safe bet that if he continues
in the MCU, Hemsworth is going to want to

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:19.920
keep charting new territory for the thunder
god.

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:22.220
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:10:22.340 --> 00:10:25.620
Plus, even more Looper videos about the MCU
are coming soon.

